WORK SESSIONS

This folder is where tamlinux-tools's work is handed from one session to the
next. It holds:

  WHATNOW        what was just done and what to do next, in detail.
                 Read it first. Every session revises it.
  W<n>-<name>    one record per work session: what was asked, what got
                 done, what failed.
  history/       the session records kept in docs/ai/ until
                 2026-11-17, unchanged and read-only.

An AI agent starting work in this repository should be able to read
WHATNOW and carry on without asking Fred to repeat himself.


WHY WORK/ AND NOT DOCS/AI

Tamlinux repositories kept session records in docs/ai/ until 2026-11-17.
Those records kept useful things (Fred's prompts, commits, test results
and the reasons behind choices) but also turned into paperwork: index
files, tables and boilerplate fields edited every session, and commits
made only to write a record's own hash into it. They had no place for
failures, and the next steps were scattered across many records.

work/ keeps the useful parts, drops the paperwork, records what failed,
and puts the next steps in one file, WHATNOW. Nothing indexes the
records, and each record goes in a commit with the work.

The earlier records moved from docs/ai/ to history/ unchanged, apart
from links rewritten to their new paths. They are read-only.

THE PROCEDURE

At the start of a session:

  1. Read this file, then WHATNOW. WHATNOW's NEXT section is the to-do
     list, in order.
  2. Read the newest record only when WHATNOW points to it or you need
     its detail:

         ls work | grep '^W' | sort -V | tail -1

At the end of a session:

  1. Write one record, W<n>-<name>, with the next number.
  2. Revise WHATNOW as a whole: update STATE, take finished items out of
     NEXT, add what this session found, and keep WAITING FOR FRED and
     WATCH OUT true. It describes the repository as it is now, not the
     history of how it got there.
  3. Commit both with the work (or with the session's last commit). If
     the session goes on after that, update the same record and WHATNOW
     instead of starting another record.


WHATNOW

Plain text, lines no longer than 76 characters, section names in
capitals:

    WHATNOW

    What tamlinux-tools is doing now and what comes next. Read this first;
    revise it at the end of every session (work/README-work).

    Updated: 2026-11-17, with W4

    STATE

      Version, branch, what works, what was just done (with W numbers).

    NEXT

      1. The next piece of work: what, where in the code, how to test
         it, and when it is done. Enough to start without asking.
      2. ...

    WAITING FOR FRED

      Builds for him to try, decisions and open points. "Nothing." if
      nothing.

    WATCH OUT

      Traps, rules and conventions that bite in this repository.

Keep it short enough to read in two minutes: details belong in plans/,
arch/ and the records, with WHATNOW pointing to them.


NAMING

Name each record W<number>-<name>, with no extension and no date: for
example W3-variables. The number gives the order. The date goes inside
the record, because git sets every file's date to when it was checked
out.


RECORD FORMAT

Plain text, no Markdown, lines no longer than 76 characters. One value
per header line. Section names in capitals.

    W3 VARIABLES AND PARAMETER EXPANSION

    Date: 2026-11-17
    AI harness: Claude Code 2.1.326
    AI model: claude-opus-5-5
    Commits: 1a2b3c4, 5d6e7f8
    Continues: W2  (only when this session picked up W2's work)

    GUIDANCE

      Fred: "Add variables next."

    DONE

      - What changed and how it was checked, one item per change.

    FAILED

      - What was tried and did not work, and why. "Nothing." if nothing.

    HANDOFF

      - Two to five lines: the headline of what is next. WHATNOW has the
        detail.

Rules:

  - Take the harness version from the running tool (claude --version,
    codex --version), never from memory.
  - Commits lists the work's commits. A record never contains the hash
    of the commit that adds it, and no commit is made just to add a hash.
  - Continues names the record whose work this session picked up. Leave
    it out otherwise.
  - Quote Fred's prompts that steered the work, word for word. Leave out
    side remarks.
  - Write only what the next agent needs and cannot get from the diff.
    15 to 40 lines is typical.


PRIVACY

This repository is public. Records and WHATNOW never contain session
IDs, transcript or store paths, home-directory paths, private repository
names or private decision numbers. Transcripts are kept privately by the
author; one private line per record maps it to its transcript.

Commits made with AI help still carry Co-authored-by, AI-Tool (with the
version) and AI-Model trailers.
